{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The cardiac cycle is the sequence of electrical and mechanical events in one heartbeat. It starts at the sinoatrial node and has two halves: systole, when the ventricles contract and eject blood, and diastole, when they relax and fill. At 72 beats a minute, one cycle lasts about 0.8 seconds, with systole about 0.3 and diastole about 0.5.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="PY5.3" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/cardiac-cycle/wiggers-diagram-overview.webp" alt="Wiggers diagram showing the ECG, aortic, ventricular and atrial pressures, ventricular volume and heart sounds aligned across one cardiac cycle." caption="A Wiggers diagram lines up the electrical and mechanical events of one heartbeat on a shared time axis." credit="DanielChangMD revised original work of DestinyQx; Redrawn as SVG by xavax, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 2.5" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiggers_Diagram.svg" width="1200" height="919" /%}

## What is the cardiac cycle?

The cardiac cycle is the sequence of electrical and mechanical events during a single heartbeat. The electrical events are shown by the electrocardiogram (ECG). The mechanical events are the changes in pressure, volume, valve position and heart sounds that follow them.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/cardiac-cycle/cardiac-conduction-system.webp" alt="Diagram of the heart showing the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres." caption="The impulse travels from the SA node to the AV node, then down the His bundle and Purkinje fibres to the ventricles." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg" width="1200" height="804" /%}

**Systole** means contraction and **diastole** means relaxation. The phases of the cycle are the same in both halves of the heart. Textbooks usually describe the left side, and the same ideas apply to the right.

The cycle begins at the **sinoatrial (SA) node**. It can generate impulses at the greatest frequency, so it is the natural pacemaker. The impulse spreads to both atria and causes atrial systole and the P wave. It then reaches the **atrioventricular (AV) node**, an area of slow conduction. This causes a normal delay between atrial and ventricular contraction, which is the PR interval. From the AV node the impulse travels down the His bundle, the left and right bundle branches and the Purkinje fibres to the ventricular muscle cells.

## Electrical events and the ECG

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/cardiac-cycle/normal-ecg-waves-intervals.webp" alt="Labelled ECG tracing of one normal beat showing the P wave, QRS complex, T wave and the PR, ST and QT intervals." caption="One normal ECG beat, with the waves and intervals used to time the electrical events." credit="CC0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SinusRhythm_withoutLabels.svg" width="1200" height="902" /%}

{% table %}
- **ECG feature**
- **What it represents**
- **Normal duration**
---
- **P wave**
- Depolarization of the atria, followed by atrial contraction
- Not given in this lesson
---
- **PR interval**
- Atrioventricular conduction, including the delay at the AV node
- Average 0.18 s, range 0.12 to 0.20 s
---
- **QRS complex**
- Depolarization of the ventricles
- Average 0.08 s, up to about 0.10 s
---
- **ST interval**
- Plateau of the ventricular action potential
- Average 0.32 s
---
- **QT interval**
- Ventricular action potential (depolarization and repolarization)
- Average 0.40 s
---
- **T wave**
- Repolarization of the ventricles, at the end of ventricular systole
- Not given in this lesson
{% /table %}

Two practical points. The QT interval varies inversely with heart rate, so it is corrected for rate (QTc). The PR interval also shortens as the heart rate rises. A standard ECG runs at 25 mm per second, so each 5 mm large square is 0.20 s and each small square is 0.04 s.

**Linking electrical and mechanical events:** atrial systole starts after the P wave. Ventricular systole starts near the end of the R wave and ends just after the T wave. Left ventricular pressure starts to build when calcium reaches the contractile proteins, shortly after the upstroke of the ventricular action potential.

## How long does each part last?

The duration of one cardiac cycle is the reciprocal of the heart rate. At 72 beats per minute, one cycle is 1/72 minute, or about 0.833 second.

- Diastole takes about **two-thirds** of the cycle and systole about **one-third**.
- At a rate of 72, each beat is about 0.8 s: about 0.3 s of systole and 0.5 s of diastole.

{% table %}
- **Heart rate**
- **Cycle**
- **Systole**
- **Diastole**
---
- **About 72 per min**
- About 0.8 s
- About 0.3 s
- About 0.5 s
---
- **200 per min**
- 0.30 s
- 0.16 s
- 0.14 s
{% /table %}

Systole is much more fixed than diastole. When the rate rises, both shorten, but diastole shortens far more. The shorter systole is mainly due to a shorter ejection phase.

## The five phases step by step

The phases are: atrial systole, isovolumetric ventricular contraction, ventricular ejection, isovolumetric ventricular relaxation, and ventricular filling. "Isovolumetric" (or isovolumic) means that the volume does not change, because all valves are shut.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/cardiac-cycle/cardiac-cycle-phases-valves.webp" alt="Series of heart diagrams showing valve positions and blood flow in each phase of the cardiac cycle." caption="Valve positions and blood flow change at each phase, with all four valves shut during the isovolumetric phases." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2027_Phases_of_the_Cardiac_Cycle.jpg" width="1200" height="1307" /%}

{% table %}
- **Phase**
- **Valves**
- **What happens**
---
- **1. Atrial systole**
- AV valves open, semilunar valves closed
- The atria contract after the P wave and add the last part of ventricular filling (the "atrial kick"). The fourth heart sound (S4) is linked to this filling.
---
- **2. Isovolumetric contraction**
- All four valves closed
- After the QRS, ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure and the AV valves close (mitral first, then tricuspid). This gives the first heart sound. Muscle tension rises but there is little or no shortening, and no emptying. It takes a further 0.02 to 0.03 s to build enough pressure to open the semilunar valves.
---
- **3. Ejection**
- Aortic and pulmonary valves open
- Ventricular pressure rises above aortic and pulmonary artery pressure and blood is ejected. Most of it leaves in the first third of ejection (rapid ejection).
---
- **4. Isovolumetric relaxation**
- All four valves closed
- The semilunar valves close, giving the second heart sound. Ventricular pressure keeps falling with no change in volume.
---
- **5. Ventricular filling**
- AV valves open, semilunar valves closed
- When ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure the AV valves open. Rapid filling follows, then a slow phase, then atrial systole starts the next cycle.
{% /table %}

## Ventricular filling in more detail

The AV valves open passively and silently in early diastole. The mitral valve opens about 30 msec before the tricuspid valve.

- **Rapid filling** follows the opening of the AV valves and accounts for most of ventricular filling. About 70 to 80% of the end-diastolic volume is reached in early and mid-diastole, well before the atria contract.
- **Diastasis** begins when ventricular and atrial pressures equalize. Filling then almost stops.
- **Atrial contraction** adds the remainder. It normally contributes about 20 to 30% of ventricular filling.

If the atria stop contracting effectively, a normal heart can cope at rest. The heart can pump 300% to 400% more blood than the resting body needs, so the loss is usually noticed only on exercise, as shortness of breath.

Stiff ventricles (with ageing, or with diseases that cause cardiac fibrosis such as high blood pressure or diabetes) fill less in early diastole. They then depend more on preload or on atrial contraction to keep cardiac output adequate. Absent atrial contraction (atrial fibrillation) or poorly timed atrial contraction can reduce ventricular filling by 20% to 30%.

## Ejection and the volumes of the ventricle

When left ventricular pressure rises slightly above 80 mm Hg (and right ventricular pressure slightly above 8 mm Hg), the semilunar valves open. About 60% of the blood in the ventricle at the end of diastole is ejected. About 70% of that leaves in the first third of ejection and 30% in the next two-thirds.

**Late in systole**, aortic pressure actually exceeds left ventricular pressure. Blood keeps flowing out for a short time because of its momentum.

Key terms:

- **End-diastolic volume (EDV):** the volume in the ventricle at the end of diastole. It can reach 150 to 180 ml in a healthy heart when a lot of blood flows in.
- **End-systolic volume (ESV):** the volume left after ejection, normally about 40 to 50 ml. With a strong contraction it can fall to 10 to 20 ml.
- **Stroke volume (SV):** the volume pumped per beat, about 70 ml from each ventricle in a resting man of average size. Increasing EDV and decreasing ESV together can more than double it.
- **Ejection fraction (EF):** the fraction of EDV that is ejected. EF = (EDV minus ESV) divided by EDV, which is the same as SV divided by EDV. It is usually about 0.6. A normal left ventricular EF is 55% or more. An EF above 50% suggests normal systolic function and below 45% suggests abnormal systolic function. It can be measured by echocardiography.
- **Cardiac output (CO):** stroke volume multiplied by heart rate. At rest it averages about 5.0 L/min (70 ml times 72 beats per minute).

## Pressures on the left and right sides

Pulmonary arterial pressure is lower than aortic pressure, so right ventricular ejection begins before left ventricular ejection, even though left ventricular electrical activation starts first. The right ventricle also reaches peak ejection later and has a lower ejection fraction (about 0.4). Over a few minutes the outputs of the two ventricles are equal.

{% table %}
- **Site**
- **Normal pressure (mm Hg)**
---
- **Right atrium**
- 0 to 8
---
- **Right ventricle**
- 15 to 30 systolic, 0 to 8 diastolic
---
- **Pulmonary artery**
- 15 to 30 systolic, 4 to 12 diastolic
---
- **Left ventricle**
- 100 to 140 systolic, 4 to 12 diastolic
---
- **Aorta**
- 100 to 140 systolic, 60 to 90 diastolic
{% /table %}

## Heart sounds and the cycle

{% table %}
- **Sound**
- **Cause**
- **Phase of the cycle**
---
- **S1**
- Closure of the mitral (M1) and tricuspid (T1) valves
- Marks the onset of ventricular systole
---
- **S2**
- Closure of the aortic (A2) and pulmonary (P2) valves
- End of ejection
---
- **S3**
- Rapid filling phase of the ventricle. Most likely caused by ventricular wall vibrations during rapid filling.
- Early diastole
---
- **S4**
- Filling of the ventricle due to atrial contraction
- Late diastole
{% /table %}

Closure of the AV valves is the normally split first heart sound, because the right ventricular pressure change is slightly delayed. The mitral component comes first.

## Splitting of the second heart sound with breathing

In expiration the aortic and pulmonary valves close at about the same time, so S2 is heard as a single sound. In inspiration the aortic valve closes slightly before the pulmonary valve, so S2 splits into A2 and P2. This is **physiological splitting**.

Why does P2 come later in inspiration?

- Inspiration lowers the intrathoracic pressure, so more blood is drawn from the venae cavae into the right atrium and ventricle.
- The right ventricle has a larger volume and only one outlet, so it takes longer to eject it.
- The pulmonary vascular bed is highly compliant and has a low resistance (10 to 20% of systemic resistance), so flow into the pulmonary artery continues for longer. The pulmonary valve closes later because of this lower impedance.

Both components move: P2 moves later and A2 moves slightly earlier, but P2 contributes more to the widening.

Bedside points: the split is easier to hear with the patient upright and breathing at a normal depth. Too deep a breath puts too much lung between the stethoscope and the heart and one or both components may vanish. Too shallow a breath may not open the split at all. A split S2 at end-expiration is so rare after age 50 that it should be considered abnormal.

## Pressure waves: aorta and atrium

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/cardiac-cycle/jugular-venous-pulse-waves.webp" alt="Graph of the jugular venous pulse showing a, c and v waves and x and y descents against the ECG." caption="Atrial pressure waves, which form the basis of the jugular venous pulse." credit="Nootherone321, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiggers_diagram_with_jugular_venous_waveform.png" width="970" height="785" /%}

**Aortic pressure wave.** The systolic upstroke follows the ECG R wave. The curve then declines and is interrupted by the **dicrotic notch** (also called the incisura), then continues to fall in diastole. In the central aorta the notch is sharp and its lowest point coincides with aortic valve closure and A2. The pulmonary artery curve has a similar notch due to pulmonary valve closure. The notch is easily recorded but is not palpable at the wrist. Normally the arterial pulse is not palpable in diastole.

**Atrial pressure waves** (the basis of the jugular venous pulse):

{% table %}
- **Wave**
- **Cause**
---
- **a wave**
- Atrial systole
---
- **c wave**
- Ventricular contraction (isovolumetric contraction, with bulging of the AV valve into the atrium)
---
- **x descent**
- Fall in pressure between the c and v waves, thought to be due to the atrium being pulled down by ventricular contraction
---
- **v wave**
- Pressure build-up from venous return before the AV valve opens
---
- **y descent**
- Fall in atrial pressure as the AV valve opens
{% /table %}

If the AV valve is incompetent, the x descent is abolished and a prominent cv wave appears.

## Pressure-volume loop

A pressure-volume loop plots left ventricular pressure against volume through one cycle. It is one of the best measures of contractility.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/cardiac-cycle/left-ventricle-pressure-volume-loop.webp" alt="Pressure-volume loop of the left ventricle with the four phases and valve events labelled." caption="The pressure-volume loop traces one cycle, and its width is the stroke volume." credit="Zachary P. Christensen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac_cycle_(pressure_volume_loop).svg" width="1200" height="757" /%}

- **A to B:** diastole, ventricular filling
- **B to C:** isovolumetric contraction
- **C to D:** ventricular emptying (ejection)
- **D to A:** isovolumetric relaxation

During isovolumetric contraction the volume stays constant while pressure rises. During ejection the volume falls. At the end of ejection the aortic valve closes and pressure falls with no change in volume. The area inside the loop represents the net external work of the ventricle.

## Two ways to define systole

- **Physiological systole** lasts from the start of isovolumic contraction (when ventricular pressure crosses atrial pressure) through the peak of the ejection phase. Physiological diastole begins as ventricular pressure starts to fall.
- **Cardiological systole** is the time from the first heart sound (M1) to aortic valve closure (A2). Everything else is cardiological diastole.

Cardiological systole starts slightly later and ends much later than physiological systole, because it is defined by sounds and not by pressures.

## Effect of heart rate

When the rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. This matters for three reasons:

1. **Filling.** Most ventricular filling happens in diastole. Filling stays adequate at moderately high rates if venous return is ample, but at very high rates filling is compromised and cardiac output per minute can fall.
2. **Coronary flow.** Blood flow to the subendocardial part of the left ventricle occurs only during diastole, when the muscle is relaxed and the small vessels are not compressed. The heart also extracts 70 to 80% of the oxygen from the blood delivered to it at rest, so it can only meet increased demand by increasing flow.
3. **Rest.** Diastole is when the heart muscle rests.

Cardiac muscle has a long action potential and cannot contract again until near the end of the first contraction. It therefore cannot be tetanized.

## What changes rate and stroke volume

- **Heart rate** is controlled mainly by the autonomic nerves. Sympathetic stimulation increases it and parasympathetic stimulation decreases it. Sinus arrhythmia is a cyclic change in heart rate with breathing (faster in inspiration, slower in expiration) due to changes in vagal tone.
- **Preload** is the degree to which the myocardium is stretched before it contracts. **Afterload** is the resistance against which blood is expelled. When heart rate and contractility stay constant, cardiac output rises with preload until very large end-diastolic volumes are reached. This is Starling's law: more stretch before contraction gives a larger stroke volume.
- **Contractility** is the strength of contraction at a given fibre length. Sympathetic stimulation increases it. The effect on rate is called a chronotropic action and the effect on strength of contraction is called an inotropic action.

## Clinical links

**Timing of murmurs.** Knowing the phases tells you when a murmur occurs:

{% table %}
- **Valve**
- **Abnormality**
- **Murmur timing**
---
- **Aortic or pulmonary**
- Stenosis
- Systolic
---
- **Aortic or pulmonary**
- Insufficiency
- Diastolic
---
- **Mitral or tricuspid**
- Stenosis
- Diastolic
---
- **Mitral or tricuspid**
- Insufficiency
- Systolic
{% /table %}

**Longer cycles.** After a ventricular premature beat (the compensatory pause), or after longer cycles in atrial fibrillation, ventricular filling increases and the next beat is stronger. Murmurs caused by obstruction to ventricular ejection become louder. Regurgitant murmurs of the AV valves do not change appreciably. This helps tell aortic stenosis from mitral regurgitation.

**Aortic insufficiency.** The pulse is strong and is called a collapsing, Corrigan or water-hammer pulse.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Remember the order:** atrial systole, isovolumetric contraction, ejection, isovolumetric relaxation, filling. Two of the five phases have **all valves closed**, and both are "isovolumetric". S1 begins the first of them, and S2 begins the second.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="S1 is produced when ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure and the mitral and tricuspid valves close. It marks the onset of ventricular systole." %}
The first heart sound is produced by:
- A. Opening of the aortic and pulmonary valves
- B. Closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves
- C. Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves
- D. Rapid ventricular filling
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="In isovolumetric contraction the AV valves have closed and the semilunar valves have not yet opened, so the ventricle contracts with no change in volume while its pressure rises." %}
Which statement is true of the period of isovolumetric ventricular contraction?
- A. Blood is ejected rapidly into the aorta
- B. All four valves are closed and ventricular volume does not change
- C. The AV valves are open
- D. Ventricular pressure falls
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Systole is more fixed than diastole. When heart rate increases, diastole shortens to a much greater degree, which can compromise ventricular filling and coronary flow to the subendocardium." %}
When the heart rate increases, which part of the cardiac cycle shortens most?
- A. Isovolumetric contraction
- B. Ejection
- C. Systole
- D. Diastole
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Inspiration increases venous return and right ventricular volume. The right ventricle has only one outlet, so it takes longer to eject the larger volume and pulmonary valve closure is delayed." %}
The pulmonary component of the second sound (P2) is delayed in inspiration mainly because:
- A. The right ventricle receives more blood and takes longer to eject it
- B. The left ventricle ejects faster
- C. The aortic valve closes later
- D. Pulmonary vascular resistance rises
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Insufficiency (regurgitation) of the aortic or pulmonary valve produces a diastolic murmur, because the valve leaks when it should be closed. Mitral insufficiency and aortic stenosis are systolic." %}
A murmur of aortic insufficiency is heard in:
- A. Systole
- B. Diastole
- C. Isovolumetric contraction only
- D. Both systole and diastole in every case
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="In the central aortic pressure wave, the lowest point of the dicrotic notch coincides with aortic valve closure and the aortic component of the second sound." %}
The dicrotic notch (incisura) of the central aortic pressure curve coincides with:
- A. Opening of the mitral valve
- B. The start of ventricular ejection
- C. Closure of the aortic valve
- D. Atrial systole
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Define the cardiac cycle." %}
The sequence of electrical and mechanical events during a single heartbeat. The electrical events are shown by the ECG, and the mechanical events are the pressure, volume, valve and sound changes that follow.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where does the cardiac cycle start and why?" %}
At the sinoatrial node, because it generates impulses at the greatest frequency and is therefore the natural pacemaker.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How long are systole and diastole at a heart rate of 72?" %}
One cycle is about 0.8 s. Systole is about 0.3 s and diastole about 0.5 s, so diastole takes about two-thirds of the cycle.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is isovolumetric contraction?" %}
The period after the AV valves close and before the semilunar valves open. Ventricular pressure and muscle tension rise but there is no change in volume because all valves are shut. It lasts a further 0.02 to 0.03 s.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Define ejection fraction and give its normal value." %}
The fraction of the end-diastolic volume that is ejected in systole, equal to stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume. It is usually about 0.6, and a normal left ventricular value is 55% or more.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Explain physiological splitting of S2." %}
In expiration A2 and P2 occur together, so S2 is single. In inspiration the right ventricle fills more and ejects for longer, and pulmonary vascular resistance is low, so P2 is delayed and S2 splits. A2 moves slightly earlier and P2 moves later, but P2 contributes more.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is a fast heart rate harmful to filling and to the heart muscle's own blood supply?" %}
Diastole shortens much more than systole, so there is less time for ventricular filling. Subendocardial coronary flow in the left ventricle occurs only during diastole, so that also falls.
{% /viva %}

